{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Phase 1 RCA — root cause PROVEN\n\n**Root cause (changeable artifact):** `tests/unit/test_path_injection_guards.py:163-166`. The\ntest hand-rolls its request as `types.SimpleNamespace(app=..., query_params={...})` — an\nINVALID stand-in for a Starlette `Request`, modelling only the query-param half of the auth\nsurface. Commit `4e8c83c833` (\"66af: stop logging webhook/rerun token\", 2026-08-04) widened\n`_request_token` (`src/rebar/review_bot/app.py:101`) to read `request.headers` FIRST and did\nnot update this fixture (`git show --stat 4e8c83c833` touches app.py, config.py and\ntests/unit/test_review_bot_token_redaction.py — NOT this file). The attribute read raises\nbefore any assertion runs.\n\n## The central question, ANSWERED WITH EVIDENCE: a header-less request is a TEST ARTIFACT, not a real production shape\n\nThree discriminating experiments, all run in-venv (starlette 1.3.1 / fastapi 0.141.1 / py3.12):\n\n**E1 — a real `Request` can NEVER raise `AttributeError` for `.headers`.**\n`starlette/requests.py:132-136` defines `headers` as an unconditional `@property`; the scope\nlookup happens inside `Headers(scope=...)` (`datastructures.py:519-522`, a bare subscript).\nObserved:\n- full scope -> `hasattr(r,'headers') is True`, type `Headers`\n- scope with `headers` key DELETED -> raises **`KeyError('headers')`**, NOT AttributeError\n\nThis is the decisive discriminator: the observed `AttributeError` is only producible by an\nobject that is not a `Request` at all. No malformed ASGI scope can produce it.\n\n**E2 — a real request with NO caller headers still has `.headers`.** Driving a FastAPI route\nannotated `request: Request` via `TestClient` with zero caller headers yields\n`{'type':'Request','mro':['Request','HTTPConnection','Mapping'],'has_headers':True,'headers_type':'Headers'}`.\n\"Header-less\" at the HTTP level means an EMPTY `Headers` object, never a missing attribute.\n\n**E3 — the production path is sound end-to-end.** `/rerun` driven through `TestClient` with\n`GerritClient.get_change_event` raising `GerritError(\"SEKRIT ... stacktrace\")`:\n- legacy query-param auth, no `X-Rebar-Token` header at all -> `502 b'{\"status\":\"gerrit error\"}'`, leak-check True\n- header auth -> `502 b'{\"status\":\"gerrit error\"}'`\n\nSupporting contract citations: the ASGI HTTP connection-scope spec lists `headers` with no\n\"Optional\" qualifier (unlike `scheme`/`raw_path`/`client`/`server`, which carry one); FastAPI\nbinds the `Request` param behind an `isinstance` gate\n(`fastapi/dependencies/utils.py:711`), whose only alternative is `WebSocket` — also an\n`HTTPConnection`, also with `.headers`; and the repo already reads headers unguarded in the\nsame shape at `src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py:135`, with no `getattr(request,\"headers\",...)`\nanywhere in `src/rebar/`.\n\n**Therefore `_request_token` must NOT be made defensive.** A `getattr(request,\"headers\",None)`\nwould catch no real input, and if the impossible condition ever did occur it would SILENTLY\ndowngrade to the legacy `?token=` query branch — exactly the log-leaking path ticket 66af\nexisted to retire. The fix belongs in the test double.\n\n`defect_legitimacy`: **defect** (in the test). Authoritative source: the ASGI scope spec plus\n`starlette/requests.py:132-136` define what a `Request` is; a double omitting a mandatory\ncontract member is invalid. The repo's own convention agrees — every other review-bot handler\ntest (`tests/unit/test_review_bot_token_redaction.py:93-155`) drives handlers through\n`fastapi.testclient.TestClient`; this hand-rolled fake is the lone outlier.\n\n**Caller-dependency gate: CLEAR.** The change is confined to one test file; nothing depends on\nthe fixture.\n\n## Correction to the report: this is NOT redding the CI Verified gate\n\nThe ticket states it reds CI. It does not — it is invisible there. The test is guarded by\n`pytest.importorskip(\"fastapi\")` (line 148) and **no CI lane ever installs fastapi**: all three\ninstall steps in `.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml` (lines 70, 219, 295) run\n`uv sync --locked --extra dev`, and the `dev` extra contains no fastapi; `reviewbot` is also\nabsent from the `specs=(...)` isolation list in `.github/workflows/_optionality.yml:169` (which\nruns an import probe only, never pytest). Verified empirically: with `--extra dev --extra agents`\nthe test reports `SKIPPED [1] ... could not import 'fastapi'`. That CI blind spot is how\n`4e8c83c833` earned `Verified +1` while breaking this test — it is a genuine second finding and\nwill be filed as a separate `discovered_from` ticket rather than fixed in this bug's scope."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786095480307981001,"uuid":"69da731d-2b39-453a-ba60-13ca54ce9b5d"}